  <div class="final-box">
    <div class="section-label" style="color: var(--gold-light)">07 · Final Recommendation</div>
    <h2>Buy — but with conditions</h2>
 
    <p>My honest assessment, combining the financial and emotional dimensions: <strong>buying land in Misiones at $30–45k USD is justified</strong>, given the current distressed market, your existing family anchor there (the Oberá school property), your long time horizon, and the strategic optionality it creates for the yerba brand.</p>
 
    <p>This is not the highest-ROI move in isolation. ETFs beat it on pure financial terms over 10 years with certainty. But you are not choosing between a purely financial life and a purely financial investment. The land gives you something no ETF can: a physical stake in a place that matters to you, with family close by, at a price that may never be this low again.</p>
 
    <p><strong>The conditions that make this a yes rather than a maybe:</strong></p>
 
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><div class="num">01</div> Buy near Oberá. Not deep interior. Not near Posadas (too expensive). The Oberá department is the sweet spot: your family is there, you know the market, and it solves the management problem informally.</li>
      <li><div class="num">02</div> Water is non-negotiable. Permanent arroyo or verified vertiente. This is the single biggest value differentiator in Misiones rural land. Walk the property in person, or send a trusted family member to verify before signing.</li>
      <li><div class="num">03</div> Get a local escribano to verify title before any offer. Not after. Occupant issues and succession title problems are common. Budget $500–1,000 for this due diligence.</li>
      <li><div class="num">04</div> Negotiate hard. Sellers are motivated. 20–30% below asking price is realistic in this market. Your $35k offer on a $48k asking price is entirely reasonable right now.</li>
      <li><div class="num">05</div> Have one family member — not a vague commitment, a named person — who agrees in writing (even informally) to check on the property twice a year, pay the inmobiliario, and alert you to anything unusual.</li>
      <li><div class="num">06</div> Do not plan for income in the first 5 years. The land banks. If something productive emerges (a neighbor wants to graze cattle, a cousin wants to plant something), great — but do not depend on it.</li>
      <li><div class="num">07</div> Confirm Wegzugsteuer treatment with your Steuerberater before purchase. Foreign real estate is generally outside scope but get it in writing before your Germany exit.</li>
    </ul>
 
    <p style="margin-top:20px; opacity:0.7; font-size:13px;"><strong>What this is not:</strong> This is not a business investment with an ROI model. It is a long-duration, USD-denominated, emotionally-rational asset purchase at a historically distressed entry point. Treat it as a 10-year hold with optionality. That's the honest framing — and on those terms, it works.</p>
